Interpreting protein-level profiles
Protein-level visualization provides a more detailed view than global summaries or clustering averages.
Individual profiles can be useful for examining:

replicate consistency;
temperature-dependent abundance changes;
differences between experimental conditions;
profiles contributing to a particular cluster;
unusual measurements that may not be apparent from dataset-level summaries.

Protein-level plots should be interpreted together with the quality-control, normalization, clustering, and model-fitting results.






ImportantProtein plots are exploratory visualizations



Visual differences between protein profiles do not by themselves establish statistical significance.
Use the statistical analyses available in TPP Analyst when evaluating significant changes in thermal behavior between experimental conditions.

Processed dataset preview
Once the protein abundance dataset and experimental design have been processed, the generated datasets can be inspected directly in TPP Analyst.
Before proceeding to downstream analysis, verify that:

protein accessions were loaded correctly;
quantitative measurements were correctly associated with the experimental design;
experimental conditions were correctly separated;
temperatures were assigned correctly;
replicates were assigned correctly;
organism assignments are correct for multi-organism experiments; and
condition and organism splitting produced the expected datasets.







ImportantCheck the processed data



Always inspect the processed dataset preview before proceeding to the Quality Control section.

Clustered data
After clustering has been performed, TPP Analyst generates a thermal-profile visualization for each cluster.
Each cluster is displayed in a separate panel.






Figure 3: Clustered data visualization.



The visualization includes:

individual protein thermal profiles;
the average thermal profile of the cluster;
the cluster identifier;
the number of proteins assigned to the cluster.

Sub-clustered trend plot
After sub-clustering, TPP Analyst generates a separate thermal-profile panel for each resulting sub-cluster.
As in the main clustering visualization, the plot contains:

individual protein thermal profiles;
an average sub-cluster thermal profile;
the sub-cluster identifier;
the number of proteins assigned to each sub-cluster.

When the original clustering was performed in comparison mode, the sub-cluster visualization retains the dataset-specific thermal profiles.
This allows smaller condition-dependent patterns to be investigated within a broader original cluster.

Running TPP analysis
After selecting the two conditions and setting the quality thresholds, click:
Run TPP analysis
TPP Analyst then:

prepares the selected condition datasets;
identifies the replicate structure and temperature points;
fits protein melting curves;
applies the selected R² and plateau quality filters;
calculates replicate-wise melting-temperature differences;
performs the statistical analysis;
generates the TPP result table and summary statistics.

Running GSEA
After selecting the ranking statistic, click:
Run GSEA
TPP Analyst:

obtains the protein ranking from the Limma results;
constructs the protein-to-GO mappings;
optionally expands the GO hierarchy;
performs the enrichment analysis using the ranked protein list.

Proteins with missing ranking values are excluded from the ranked list.
